{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Live rich-text cutover + state echo suppression + LWW conflict safety\n\n## Context / Problem\nThis story **cuts the live Cloud and DC send paths over to the now-capable rich\ncodecs** (delivered by `e59d` and `271c`, on which this story depends) and makes\nthat cutover echo-safe. Because the DC codec is one-way and lossy, loop prevention\ncannot rely on `decode(encode(x))==x`. Both clients must suppress echo by\n**state**: compare the current Jira value against the last-synced baseline the\nreconciler already keeps (ADR 0026, `binding_store.py:102`; `_BASELINE_FIELDS` at\n`:108`; `set_baseline` at `:459`). We must also not silently destroy either side's\nedits under concurrency, and any state we add must respect epic `0303`'s churn\ndiscipline (a per-pass timestamp there caused the tickets-store bloat this feature\nmust not regress).\n\n**Why the cutover lives HERE, not in the leaves.** `e59d`/`271c` deliver capable\ncodecs but deliberately leave the live send sites on the plain/identity path, so\nthat the observable format change and its echo-safety land **together** in one\nstory (this one), never a hazard-before-safety window.\n\n## Prior art in this repo (build on the existing convergence, do NOT re-invent it)\n- The rich-text COMPARE normalization already exists as the codec's\n  **`normalize_outbound`** op (`RichTextCodec`, `adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py`).\n  The description diff already routes every side through it / the equivalent\n  `adf.normalize_description`: `OutboundFieldMapper.map_fields_to_remote`\n  (`outbound_mapper.py:77`), `outbound_fields.py:564`, and `inbound_differ.py:210`\n  (Jira side at `:212`). This story adds the three-way BASELINE layer ON TOP of\n  that convergence; it does NOT add a second, parallel `norm()`.\n- `binding_store.py` already persists the last-synced baseline (ADR 0026); this\n  story extends that record, it does not create a new store.\n- **There is already exactly ONE baseline writer:** `_advance_baselines`\n  (`reconcile.py:519`), run **always-on** in the persist phase (`:600`) and reading\n  `ctx.curr_snapshot` — the START-of-pass fetch — to advance every confirmed\n  binding's baseline (story d6bd, ADR 0026). This story does **not** add a second,\n  parallel baseline writer; doing so would let the persist-phase advance overwrite\n  it with the stale start-of-pass value. Instead it feeds the post-push value INTO\n  that single existing writer (see Approach).\n- The codec law `normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t))`\n  (`test_rich_text_codec.py:163`) and the comment dedup that depends on it\n  (`outbound_comments.py:48`) are preserved by routing every compare through the\n  codec op.\n\n## The live cutover (both clients — this story's new scope)\nReroute the direct plain-encode call sites through the capable codecs so the wire\nactually carries rich text, and prove convergence:\n- **Cloud:** repoint the four `AdfCodec` ops to the Markdown-aware `adf.py`\n  functions (`to_wire→markdown_to_adf`, `decode_inbound→adf_to_markdown`,\n  `normalize_outbound→adf_to_markdown∘markdown_to_adf`,\n  `fit_outbound→fit_markdown_to_adf_limit`) and install the whole-codec plain-text\n  fallback, updating the pin test at `test_rich_text_codec.py:198/205/207/208`.\n  Then reroute the live consumers to the Markdown-aware path: the encode at\n  `adapters/jira/acli.py:205` and `acli_cli_ops.py:369`, the fit at\n  `jira_fields.py:53`, and the direct `adf.normalize_description` /\n  `adf.fit_text_to_adf_limit` compare sites at `outbound_fields.py:564` and\n  `inbound_differ.py:210` — **paired so the send-fit and the compare-fit are the\n  identical transform** (the once-only re-emit convergence invariant). Each\n  already-synced description re-emits **exactly once** (the `outbound_fields.py:564`\n  once-only re-emit mechanism) to upgrade to rich ADF, then converges.\n- **DC:** wire the pure `wiki_render.render_markdown_to_wiki` (from `271c`) into\n  `WikiTextCodec.to_wire`, and **redefine `WikiTextCodec.normalize_outbound` to the\n  compare form `decode_inbound(to_wire(t))`** so the codec law\n  (`test_rich_text_codec.py:163`) is re-established for the rendering codec. Because\n  DC's live send composes `normalize_outbound(fit_outbound(value))` at\n  `outbound_mapper.py:77`, this redefinition is exactly what makes\n  `issue.update(fields=…)` begin shipping rendered wiki — the cutover — so it\n  **must** land here with echo-safety, not in the leaf. **The DC CREATE path is a\n  SEPARATE, explicitly-enumerated codec consumer:** `_map_local_to_dc_fields`\n  (`backend.py:70`) constructs its OWN `WikiTextCodec()` and builds\n  `\"description\": codec.fit_outbound(...)`, applying **only `fit_outbound`, never\n  `to_wire`** — so redefining `to_wire`/`normalize_outbound` alone does NOT render\n  on create. This story changes `_map_local_to_dc_fields` to render before the fit\n  (compose the renderer/`to_wire` with `fit_outbound`) so a newly-created DC issue\n  ships rendered wiki too. Update the DC identity pin tests\n  (`test_wiki_normalize_is_identity`,\n  `test_wiki_codec_wire_shape_and_decode_are_the_plain_string`,\n  `test_non_string_values_pass_through_untouched`) to the rendering behavior.\n- **Rollback:** the cutover is gated behind a single switch (config/flag) that\n  restores the plain/identity path for that client without reverting the codec\n  capability, so a bad rollout is one revert, not a code rewrite.\n\n## Approach (echo-safety + concurrency)\nDrive body sync from a three-way state — `baseline` (last-synced Jira canonical\nform), `local` (rebar body), `remote` (current Jira value) — each normalized\nthrough the **existing codec `normalize_outbound`** (compare-only) before diffing.\nOutbound fires only when `local` differs from `baseline` (rebar-originated\nchange); a value equal to `baseline` is our own echo and is skipped. **The\nbaseline must be the value Jira holds AFTER our push, not the start-of-pass\nsnapshot** — a benign Jira re-canonicalization of rich text (ADF/wiki) would\notherwise be misread next pass as a human edit. We achieve this **without a second\nbaseline writer**: the reconciler already advances baselines in exactly one place —\nthe persist-phase `_advance_baselines` (`reconcile.py:600`→`:519`), which reads\n`ctx.curr_snapshot`. In the apply phase (`_apply_mutations`), we consume the\n**per-action apply results** — each `apply_handlers.HandlerResult.outcome` carries\nthe mutation's `key`/`local_id` plus an `error` field and a `soft_failed` marker —\nselect the description-update actions whose outcome **succeeded** (`error is None`\nand not `soft_failed`), re-GET the post-push canonical value for exactly those\nJira keys, and **merge it into `ctx.curr_snapshot` for that key** (one extra GET,\nonly on a successfully-changed field; a FAILED or soft-failed apply is never\nobserved and never advances its baseline). So the single existing\n`_advance_baselines` at persist records the post-push canonical form for\nsuccessfully-mutated fields and the unchanged start-of-pass value for the rest. This places the observe-after-push GET explicitly in the pass data-flow\n(post-apply, before the persist-phase advance reads the snapshot) and removes the\ncollision the always-on advance would otherwise cause. When both `local` and\n`remote` diverged from `baseline` (concurrent edit), apply last-write-wins to pick\nthe winner but always emit a detectable conflict signal so nothing is silently\nlost.\n\n**Origin-gating so Jira-native constructs survive (AC5).** Outbound is gated on\nrebar-origin: a description update fires only when `local` differs from `baseline`\n(a rebar-side edit). When a Jira author adds a native construct with no Markdown\nsource (a panel/mention/date — constructs `adf.py` already decodes at `:164/219`)\nbetween passes and rebar made no edit, that pass sees `remote != baseline` but\n`local == baseline`, so origin-gating emits **no** outbound and the construct\nsurvives untouched. Only a genuine rebar-originated body change re-encodes (and a\nconcurrent both-sides edit is surfaced as the LWW conflict above, never a silent\nclobber). For the rich layer, store a\nchange-gated `rich_sha = sha256(rendered_wire)[:16]` (8 bytes) **inline** in the\nbinding (proven 0.07 KB/version stable, identical to the baseline profile; a\nper-pass timestamp was proven to reproduce 0303's 12.62 KB anti-pattern) so a\nre-render is only pushed when the wire actually changed. The compare stays\ncomparison-only and never writes back.\n\n## Scope\n- `adapters/jira/rich_text_codec.py` — Cloud cutover: repoint the four `AdfCodec`\n  ops to the Markdown-aware `adf.py` functions + install the whole-codec fallback.\n- `adapters/jira/acli.py` (`:205`), `adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py` (`:369`),\n  `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py` (`:53`), `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`\n  (`:564`), `inbound_differ.py` (`:210`) — Cloud live cutover through the\n  Markdown-aware path (gated).\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_rich_text_codec.py` (`:198/205/207/208`) —\n  update the Cloud pin test to the Markdown-aware delegation; update the DC identity\n  pin tests + codec-law test for the now-rendering `WikiTextCodec`.\n- `adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py` — DC cutover: `WikiTextCodec.to_wire`\n  delegates to `wiki_render.render_markdown_to_wiki`; `normalize_outbound` set to\n  `decode_inbound(to_wire(t))` (re-establishes the codec law for the rendering\n  codec) — this is what makes `outbound_mapper.py:77` ship rendered wiki.\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` (`:70`) — DC CREATE-path: change\n  `_map_local_to_dc_fields` (today `codec.fit_outbound(...)` only) to render via the\n  renderer/`to_wire` before the fit, so create ships rendered wiki (gated).\n- `binding_store.py` — inline change-gated `rich_sha` beside the existing baseline\n  (`_BASELINE_FIELDS`).\n- `reconcile.py` (`_apply_mutations` ~`:370`) — after apply, re-GET the post-push\n  canonical value for each mutated Jira key and merge it into `ctx.curr_snapshot`\n  so the single existing persist-phase `_advance_baselines` (`:519`/`:600`) records\n  the post-push form; **no second baseline writer is added**.\n- the existing description differ / compare path — three-way baseline diff + LWW\n  arbitration + **origin-gating** (outbound only when `local != baseline`, so a\n  Jira-native construct authored with no rebar edit survives), reusing the codec\n  `normalize_outbound` (NOT a new `norm()`, NOT changing the shared plain-text\n  `inbound_fields.normalize_rich_text`).\n- `tests/` — cutover, convergence, echo, conflict, and no-churn tests.\n\n## Testing\n- **Cloud convergence:** after the cutover, a converted ADF description posts once,\n  and a second reconcile pass emits **zero** description updates (`test_cloud_cutover_converges`).\n- **DC convergence:** a rendered-wiki description posts once and a second pass\n  emits zero description updates (`test_dc_cutover_converges`).\n- Self-echo: a rebar-originated body change propagates exactly once and a second\n  pass pushes nothing (0 echo-back).\n- Concurrent: both sides edit the same body; the pass records a conflict and\n  applies LWW without discarding the loser silently (799/799-detected invariant).\n- Origin-gating: a Jira author adds a native panel/mention to a synced description\n  and rebar makes no edit; the next pass emits zero outbound and the construct\n  survives verbatim (`test_jira_native_construct_survives`).\n- No-churn: a pass with no body change updates 0 `rich_sha` entries and writes no\n  timestamp; asserted by the git-delta cost harness (≤0.07 KB/version stable).\n- Rollback: with the cutover flag off, the wire reverts to the plain/identity\n  encode for that client (`test_cutover_rollback_restores_plain`).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] After the Cloud cutover, a converted ADF description posts once and a second\n  reconcile pass emits zero description updates; verified by\n  `test_cloud_cutover_converges`.\n- [ ] After the DC cutover, `issue.update(fields=…)` receives rendered wiki (not\n  raw Markdown) and a second pass emits zero description updates; verified by\n  `test_dc_cutover_converges`.\n- [ ] The three-way diff reuses the codec `normalize_outbound` for the compare and\n  adds no parallel normalizer; verified by `test_echo_uses_codec_normalize`.\n- [ ] Outbound fires only on a rebar-originated change and the baseline stored is\n  the value observed after push, fed through the single existing\n  `_advance_baselines` via a post-apply `curr_snapshot` merge (no second baseline\n  writer); verified by `test_echo_observed_after_push` (a benign Jira\n  re-canonicalization does not re-sync).\n- [ ] A Jira-authored native construct (panel/mention/date) survives a subsequent\n  rebar-originated reconcile pass under origin-gating; verified by\n  `test_jira_native_construct_survives`.\n- [ ] A concurrent both-sides edit is detected and flagged under LWW with the\n  losing side never silently destroyed; verified by\n  `test_concurrent_conflict_detected` (799/799 over the corpus sample).\n- [ ] The rich layer stores a change-gated inline 8-byte `rich_sha` and no\n  per-pass timestamp, changing 0 entries on a no-op pass; verified by the\n  git-delta cost harness reproducing the ≤0.07 KB/version stable profile.\n- [ ] The cutover is flag-gated and reverts to the plain/identity wire when off;\n  verified by `test_cutover_rollback_restores_plain`."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785813612359412001,"uuid":"b6a2a99d-2406-4faa-8a55-808a268b95f2"}